---
title: Remote
order: 3
description: Remote repositories, push, pull, fetch, and upstream tracking
tags: [git, remote, push, pull, fetch, upstream, origin]
---

Remotes are named references to external repositories. Origin is the
conventional name for the primary remote.

@code bash
# List remotes
git remote
git remote -v                    # show URLs

# Add and manage remotes
git remote add origin git@github.com:user/repo.git
git remote add upstream https://github.com/original/repo.git
git remote rename origin old-origin
git remote remove upstream
git remote set-url origin git@github.com:user/new-repo.git
git remote show origin           # detailed remote info

@text
Fetch downloads objects and refs from remotes without modifying the
working tree. Pull is fetch + merge (or rebase).

@code bash
# Fetch
git fetch                        # fetch from origin
git fetch --all                  # fetch from all remotes
git fetch origin main            # fetch specific branch
git fetch --prune                # remove stale remote-tracking branches
git fetch --tags                 # fetch all tags

# Pull (fetch + merge)
git pull
git pull origin main
git pull --rebase                # fetch + rebase instead of merge
git pull --rebase=interactive    # interactive rebase on pull

@text
Push uploads local commits to a remote. Set up tracking so future
push/pull commands know the default remote and branch.

@code bash
# Push
git push
git push origin main
git push origin feature-auth
git push --all                   # push all branches
git push --tags                  # push all tags

# Set upstream tracking
git push -u origin feature-auth  # push and track
git branch --set-upstream-to=origin/main main

# Check tracking
git branch -vv                   # show tracking branches

# Force push (use with care)
git push --force-with-lease      # safer: fails if remote has new commits
git push --force                 # overwrites remote (dangerous)

# Push a tag
git tag v1.0
git push origin v1.0
git push origin --tags           # push all tags

# Delete remote branch/tag
git push origin --delete feature-auth
git push origin --delete v0.9    # delete remote tag

@text
Common fork workflow: track both origin (your fork) and upstream
(original repo).

@code bash
# Set up fork tracking
git remote add upstream https://github.com/original/repo.git
git fetch upstream

# Sync fork with upstream
git checkout main
git fetch upstream
git merge upstream/main          # or: git rebase upstream/main
git push origin main

# Create PR branch from upstream
git fetch upstream
git switch -c fix-typo upstream/main
